<article class="env-lemma" id="051J">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/051J">Lemma <span data-tag="051J">10.101.5</span>.</a> Let $\varphi : R \to R'$ be a ring map. Let $I \subset R$ be an ideal. Let $M$ be an $R$-module. Assume </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$I$ is nilpotent, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$R \to R'$ is injective, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$M/IM$ is flat over $R/I$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$R' \otimes _ R M$ is flat over $R'$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Then $M$ is flat over $R$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Define inductively $I_1 = I$ and $I_{n + 1} = \varphi ^{-1}(\varphi (I_ n)^2R')$ for $n \geq 1$. Note that by Lemma <a href="/tag/051I" data-tag="051I">10.101.4</a> we find that $M/I_ nM$ is flat over $R/I_ n$ for each $n \geq 1$. It is clear that $\varphi (I_{n + 1}) \subset \varphi (I)^{2^ n}R'$. Since $I$ is nilpotent we see that $\varphi (I_ n) = 0$ for some $n$. As $\varphi $ is injective we conclude that $I_ n = 0$ for some $n$ and we win. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>